  "worked_solution": "First, let's make a diagram of the situation:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14196324/assets/513739a7ddcd73dab6cdf5374d24da54cf062e45ddf1b5b3985890e04a7cacdd.png)\n\nLet $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y=32-\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{6}.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA=xy=x(32-\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{6})=32x-\\frac{4}{7}{x}^{7}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=32-4{x}^{6},\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 32-4{x}^{6} & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{6} & =32 \\\\ {x}^{6} & =8 \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,\\sqrt{2})$ | $\\sqrt{2}$ | $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt[6]{7})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $1.5$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo $x=\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(\\sqrt{2}) & =32(\\sqrt{2})-\\frac{4}{7}(\\sqrt{2})^{7} \\\\  & =32\\sqrt{2}-\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{192\\sqrt{2}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
